#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the gates a repository subscribes to.

The manifest is a plain text file, one gate per line:

    # .vibe-verifier
    no-duplicate-package-json-keys
    new-source-has-test --source 'lib/**/*.ts' --soak
    branch-name-length --max 37

The aggregate follows the gate contract: exit 2 if any gate could not run, else
exit 1 if any gate found violations, else 0. A missing or empty manifest is
exit 2: a run that verified nothing must not look like a pass. A gate whose pass
checked less than it could leaves a note (`qualify` in gates/_contract.py), and its
summary row reads `PASS (<note>)`.

On a pull request the manifest is judged from the base: the run's entries are the union of
the base's and the head's gates, and a gate in both takes the base's arguments. A PR that adds a
gate is bitten by it now; one that removes a gate, appends --soak or raises a limit is still
judged by the line it started from. The base is resolved the way the gates resolve theirs
(--base-ref, then $VIBE_VERIFIER_BASE_REF, $GITHUB_BASE_REF, main, master, HEAD~1); with no
base, or no manifest at the base, the head manifest stands alone (a first subscription, a
laptop with no history). The workflow file itself is still the head's, so this is a guard
against a PR quietly weakening its own manifest, not a platform guarantee. Every gate runs with
$VIBE_VERIFIER_MANIFEST set to the manifest file the run read (the head's): `repo-rules --doc`
lists the rules that manifest enables.

With `--manifest`, `criteria` runs its subscribed `ticket-coverage` line before deciding whether
a browser walk is needed. The checkout must have full Git history; coverage is never skipped
by `- None:`, unrendered paths or a carried walk. Findings go to stderr to preserve its output protocol.

`consumers` is the inventory and drift check for subscribed repositories. It reads
each repository's manifest, its workflow stub, and every other workflow under
.github/workflows/ that pins a catalog action (a harness copy such as qae-explore.yml)
through `gh api` (read-only) and reports, per repository: whether each pinned commit is
current, stale (a commit since it touched gates/, bin/, actions/, consumer/, tools/ or rules/), or unknown
to this repository's history; whether the stub matches consumer/vibe-verifier.yml outside
the pin line and the `runs-on:` values (each job's runner is the consumer's choice, and a pin bump
keeps it); whether every gate in the manifest exists; and the native enforcement GitHub
holds for the repository (SHA pinning, the actions allowlist, Dependabot alerts), which no
workflow token can read, so this inventory is where a revert to `all` surfaces. The source
repository gets a row too, with its stub and manifest checks skipped: it carries neither by
design, and its native state is watched like any consumer's. Exit 2 if any repository could
not be read, 1 if anything needs action, else 0.

With `--wrapper PATH` (a checkout of an organization's CI repository that subscribes its
repositories to the catalog) the inventory has three more kinds of pin. The wrapper gets a row of
kind `wrapper`: no stub is expected, every catalog pin in its workflows is judged like a
consumer's, and a review.yml copy is compared under `jobs:` only, with the token secret named
alike and its verify step's gate list left out (the wrapper owns its header, its secret and that
list). Every ruleset of the wrapper's organization whose `workflows` rule requires a wrapper
workflow gets a row: each pinned commit is current or stale against the wrapper's --wrapper-ref
(stale when a commit since it touched that workflow), a bare ref is unpinned, and a ruleset that is
not active needs action. The repositories it targets by name are subscribers with no stub
(`stub: ruleset`) and no manifest of their own: their gate list is the wrapper's entry for them,
repos/<name>/vibe-verifier in the wrapper at --wrapper-ref, which must exist, and a .vibe-verifier
or .vibe-verifier-review left in such a repository is stray, since nothing reads it. The wrapper's
own row is judged the same way: its own pull requests run its gate workflow. A `uses:` of a
wrapper workflow in any repository's workflows is judged the same way, so a caller of a legacy
reusable workflow is listed even with no manifest.

`apply-down` bumps stale pins, and nothing else. It is two-stage on purpose: with no
`--confirm` it reads the same inventory and prints a plan plus a digest of exactly what
it would write; `--confirm <digest>` opens one pull request per repository, changing only
pin lines (one commit per workflow file). It never merges, never touches a repository whose
stub has drifted, and never opens a second pull request for a bump that is already open; an open
bump to an older commit is closed as superseded by the new one. A plan whose digest no longer
matches is refused rather than applied, so a bump is reviewed as the thing it was. `--auto` is the
scheduled form (actions/pin-bump, gates/_pin_bump.py): it holds a repository whose bump moves a tool
version or drops an input a workflow passes (exit 1), moves no ruleset, and confirms its own digest only with `--act` and when it is
time: `--now`, the oldest waiting release commit `--overdue-hours` old, or a quiet `--lane`. With `--wrapper PATH` it bumps both levels in the same plan: the wrapper's
catalog pins in a pull request to the wrapper, each caller's pins of wrapper workflows in a pull
request of its own, and each ruleset's pins with a PUT of that ruleset's rules, where only the
stale entries' `sha` moves to the wrapper's head. A ruleset moves to what is on the wrapper's
--wrapper-ref, so run it again once the wrapper's own bump has merged.

`tool <name>` prints the directory where the node toolchain pinned by tools/<name>/package-lock.json
is installed (installing it first when the cache does not have that lockfile's digest), so a
workflow step can run a pinned toolchain the same way a gate does. Exit 2 when there is no such
toolchain or the install fails.

`criteria <file> [--changed-files FILE]` reads a PR body with the acceptance-verdict grammar: its
criteria are the list under its `Acceptance criteria` heading, and a body without that heading lists
none. Its first line is `none: <reason>` when it needs no browser check, else `criteria: <n>`. It needs none when it declares `- None: <why>`, or, given the pull request's
changed paths one per line, when it lists no criteria and every path is CI configuration or
documentation no site renders. A harness uses it to skip work, so the decision is made by the same
code in the job that explores and the job that judges. After `criteria: <n>` it prints one line per
annotation a criterion carries (`AC2 references: home-desktop`, `AC3 roles: admin, read-only`,
`AC4 malformed: [ref: ]`), read the way the acceptance-verdict gate reads them. With `--ticket FILE`,
the criteria of the ticket the pull request implements (written by the consumer's workflow) count too,
as TC1, TC2, ...: a ticket that lists criteria, or a non-empty one in which none can be found (printed
as `ticket: ...`, and refused by the gate), makes a check needed whatever the pull request declares, so
it cannot drop its ticket's requirements; an empty file links no ticket. A ticket criterion the body
proves with a `ticket-proof: TCn -- ...` line is not walked: it is left out of the count and printed as
`TCn proven by a test, not walked` (acceptance-verdict judges the proof). With `--max-shards N` it then
prints `shards: [1,2,...]`, the explorers the criteria are split across, for a harness to run in parallel:
one walk is a criterion as one role it names at one width, an explorer is sized for six, and there are
never more than N explorers nor than chains of criteria. A criterion is never split, and criteria that build
on each other are a chain one explorer walks whole: a criterion that names another of its list by number
("on the profile from item 2", "after AC3") is chained to it. A `walks: <n>` line says how many it counted,
and a `chain: AC2, AC3, ...` line names each chain. Which criteria each explorer walks is `qae-inputs --shard`'s to say: it also knows the
feature re-walk, which this command does not. The widths are the `--widths` of the qae-artifacts
line of `--manifest FILE`, judged from the base like a run (no manifest counts one width). More walks
than the explorer with the most of them is sized for prints one `::warning::` with the numbers. Exit 1, printing nothing on
stdout and one `::error::` line per problem on stderr, when the body holds criteria no run can check: a
heading that names acceptance criteria but is not exactly `## Acceptance criteria` (with no criteria under
the exact one), a criterion judged against main or production, which QAE never opens, or, given
`--roles admin, rep` (the roles the preview has a login for, as the site step names them), a criterion
whose `[as: ...]` names any other role. Run it on a PR body before pushing. Exit 2 when a file cannot be read.

`qae-inputs [--manifest FILE] [--widths-file FILE] [--environmental-file FILE] [--references DIR] [--evidence DIR] [--listing FILE]
[--digests FILE] [--turns FILE [--criteria FILE] [--ticket FILE] [--features DIR] [--shard K --shards N]
[--share FILE] [--share-ids FILE] [--run ID] [--isolation FILE]]` prepares what the QAE
explorer and its gates read from the site step's supplies and the QAE manifest, before the explorer runs.
Each design reference `<key>.png` the site step wrote to DIR (default qae-inputs/references) is copied
to `<evidence>/references/<key>.png` (default qae-artifacts), listed with its size in FILE (default
qae-inputs/references.md) for the explorer, and its sha256 written to the digests file as one JSON object,
which the workflow declares as an output so the gate judges the image the workflow supplied and never one
the explorer wrote. Given the manifest, the `--widths` of its qae-artifacts line (judged from the base,
like a run) are written one per line to the widths file (default qae-inputs/widths), so the explorer
checks each criterion at the widths the gate will require; no such option writes no file. Its
`--allow-console` and `--allow-request` patterns, from the same line, are written to the environmental file
(`--environmental-file`, default qae-inputs/environmental), one `console: <regex>` or `request: <regex>` line
each, so the explorer never fails a criterion on a console message or request the gate does not judge; none
writes no file. With
`--turns FILE` it also writes the explorer's turn cap there, sized to its walks: each criterion of the PR
body (`--criteria`, default qae-inputs/pr-body.md) and of the ticket (`--ticket`, default
qae-inputs/ticket.md; absent is none) once per role it names and per width, plus each feature to re-walk
(`--features`, default qae-inputs/features), which is one walk per three states the manifest's
acceptance-verdict line lets the explorer walk of it (`--max-states`, default 3). With `--shard K --shards N`
the explorer is one of N that share the criteria (`criteria --max-shards` decided N). Largest walks first, each
criterion, or chain of criteria that build on each other, goes whole to the explorer with the fewest walks so far, the lowest-numbered on a tie, and the first
explorer starts with the walks of the feature re-walk, which only it does, so it takes fewer criteria and
reaches the re-walk in its time. Every explorer counts the features the same way, to make the same
assignment; equal-sized criteria and each final share keep document order and original criterion numbers.
For one past the first `--features DIR` and its listing are then removed: it is not handed
them. The cap is sized to the explorer's own share, and `--share FILE` gets the one sentence that names that
share for its prompt (an empty line for one explorer), and `--share-ids FILE` the ids of the criteria it walks, as a JSON
list, for the step that keeps its evidence (every criterion for one explorer). For one of several the sentence also names the chains in its share,
to walk in order, and the explorer's tag, qae-<run>-e<K> (`--run ID`, the run id and attempt): every record the
explorer creates carries it, and it opens or judges only records that carry it or that existed before the run,
never one another explorer of the run created or is changing. `--isolation FILE` gets what the browser holds it
to (actions/qae-browser/console-pages.js), as JSON: `tag`, and `protected`, every record id (a UUID, 24 hex
digits or a ULID) a criterion outside its share names, which it may not delete; for one explorer the file is
removed. It prints `references: <n>`, one
`<key>: <width>x<height>` line each, `widths: <list>` (or `widths: none`), `environmental: <n>` and, with `--turns`,
`max turns: <n> (<walks> walks)` and, for one explorer of several, `share: <ids> (explorer K of N)`. Exit 2
when a supplied file is not a PNG or its name is not a reference key, the manifest or the criteria file
cannot be read, or the explorer has nothing to walk (the criteria changed after they were counted).

`features --manifest FILE --changed-files FILE [--out DIR [--listing FILE]]` reads the manifest's
acceptance-verdict line (judged from the base, like a run) and, when it has `--features`, prints which
features of that feature map the changed paths touch: the same selection the gate requires a
`regression-check:` line for (gates/_features.py). Its first line is `features: <n>`; with `--out` it
copies each selected feature file, as the working tree has it (the pull request's own copy, so one that
updates a feature is re-walked against its updated Reach and Verify), to `<DIR>/<id>.md` for a QAE
explorer to read (a feature's `re-walk:` line notes it when the pull request changed its Verify section
since the merge base, gates/_verify_edits.py), and with `--listing` writes the list the explorer reads beside them: the most states to
walk of each feature (the line's `--max-states`, default 3) and the changed paths that selected each. A
line without `--features` prints `features: 0` and the reason, and so does a branch that predates the
map (no feature file at the head or at its merge base with the base, and some at the base): it has no
feature to re-walk until it takes the base. Exit 2 when the manifest, the changed paths or the map
cannot be read, which includes every other head with no feature file.

`rules-doc` renders the Markdown block that lists every rule the manifest's `repo-rules` lines enable
(their packs and rule directories, read from the working tree): each rule's id, whether it blocks or
is advisory (as the gate judges it), its language and globs, and its message, sorted by id, between
BEGIN and END comment lines that say it is generated. It prints the block, or with `--write PATH`
replaces the block in that file, appending it when the file has none. `repo-rules --doc PATH` fails a
pull request whose block differs from what this renders for its HEAD.

`qa-review` posts the QAE's one review comment on a pull request, or edits it in place when it is
already there: passed, changes needed, not required, could not run, or not walked (when `--limits`, from
`qa-limit`, says every explorer hit a model usage limit: the head is recorded as limited), with the head it judged,
each criterion's verdict from the explorer (and each re-walked feature's, noting a feature whose Verify section the
pull request changed, read from the map `--manifest` names in `--checkout`), and a link to the run. The state comes
from what the workflow's jobs and gates concluded, never from the explorer's prose.

`qa-limit --repo OWNER/NAME --run-id ID --head SHA` reads the failure annotations of a run's explore jobs and, when
every one failed on a model usage limit, warns with each limit and its reset and writes `limited=true` and the limits
to $GITHUB_OUTPUT: the QAE verify job then skips the steps that judge a walk and passes with that warning. Any other
failure, a server-side rate limit among them, an explorer that succeeded, or annotations it cannot read write
`limited=false`. A failed explore job whose steps or annotations GitHub has not listed yet is read again every
`--interval` seconds (15) for up to `--wait` (420), and a job a rerun copied is read as the attempt that ran it.

`proof-audit --body FILE [--model M] [--reasoning-effort E] [--max-ids N] [--grades DIR]` grades, with a model
on the Codex CLI (tools/codex) under a ChatGPT-managed login ($CODEX_HOME, default ~/.codex), whether the
repository's tests prove each requirement the sections the body's `Ticket:` lines link define, and, with
`--plans-endpoint URL` (the token in $VV_PLANS_TOKEN), the whole wording of each plan a `[plan:<id>]` marker in
the body or `--title` names in a plan store, less the IDs its `Split off:` lines give to another owner (one
that is `--pr`, this pull request's number, cannot run), never showing
the model the body, then runs the proof-audit gate on the grades and exits with its code: the same prompt,
schema and gate the QAE verify job runs, so a session can see the verdict before it pushes. `--plan OUT` and
`--requirements PLAN --shard K` are CI's two halves of it (harnesses/proof-audit/README.md).

`copy-audit --artifacts DIR [--site-file FILE] [--grade-only] [--all]` grades, with a model on the same Codex
CLI and login, whether each sentence and label in the copy record the QAE browser hook wrote (DIR/copy.jsonl,
or `node actions/qae-browser/ui-audit.js --copy DIR/copy.jsonl URL...` from a shell) is plain English, writes
copy-grades.json beside it, then runs the copy-audit gate and exits with its code. `--grade-only` is the explore
job's half (harnesses/copy-audit/README.md).

`affected-tests [--base-ref REF] [--tests GLOB] [--exclude GLOB] [--always GLOB] [--all-on GLOB] [--report RESULTS]` prints the
test files a pull request's changes can affect, one per line, or `ALL`, with a summary on stderr, for the
repository's own test job to run (gates/_affected_tests.py has the rule). It is not a gate: exit 0 with a
selection, 2 when it cannot run. Without a base (`--base-ref`, then $VIBE_VERIFIER_BASE_REF, then
$GITHUB_BASE_REF, which only a pull request sets) or with one it cannot read, it prints `ALL`. With `--report`
(a JUnit XML report or a list of failed test files, from the full suite the job also ran) it prints instead each
test that failed there and was not selected.
"""
import argparse
import os
import sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(ROOT, "gates"))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(ROOT, "bin"))  # bin/cli/: every subcommand but the three run from gates/ below
import _affected_tests  # noqa: E402
import _copy_audit  # noqa: E402
import _merge_grant_cli  # noqa: E402
import _proof_audit  # noqa: E402
from cli import apply_down, consumers, criteria, features, qa_limit, qa_review, qae_inputs, rules_doc, runner  # noqa: E402

COMMANDS = {
    "run": runner.run,
    "list": runner.list_command,
    "consumers": consumers.consumers,
    "apply-down": apply_down.apply_down,
    "tool": runner.tool_command,
    "criteria": criteria.criteria_command,
    "qae-inputs": qae_inputs.qae_inputs_command,
    "features": features.features_command,
    "rules-doc": rules_doc.rules_doc_command,
    "affected-tests": _affected_tests.command,
    "proof-audit": _proof_audit.command,
    "copy-audit": _copy_audit.command,
    "merge-grant": _merge_grant_cli.command,
    "qa-review": qa_review.qa_review_command,
    "qa-limit": qa_limit.qa_limit_command,
}


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="vibe-verifier", description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    commands = parser.add_subparsers(dest="command", required=True)
    runner.add_run_arguments(commands.add_parser("run", help="run the subscribed gates"))
    commands.add_parser("list", help="list the gates in this catalog")
    consumers.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("consumers", help="inventory and drift check of subscribed repositories "
                                                "(read-only, via gh)"))
    apply_down.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("apply-down", help="open one pull request per consumer bumping a stale "
                                                 "action pin"))
    runner.add_tool_arguments(commands.add_parser("tool", help="install (once per lockfile digest) and print the "
                                                  "directory of a node toolchain under tools/"))
    criteria.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("criteria", help="read a criteria document: prints `none: <reason>` or "
                                               "`criteria: <n>`"))
    qae_inputs.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("qae-inputs", help="copy the site step's design references into the "
                                                 "QAE evidence, list them for the explorer and write their digests"))
    features.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("features", help="the features a pull request's changed paths touch, by "
                                               "the selection on the manifest's acceptance-verdict line: prints "
                                               "`features: <n>`"))
    rules_doc.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("rules-doc", help="print, or write into a file, the generated block "
                                                "listing the rules the manifest's repo-rules lines enable"))
    _affected_tests.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("affected-tests", help="the test files a pull request's changes can "
                                                      "affect, one per line, or ALL, for the repository's test job to "
                                                      "run (not a gate)"))
    _proof_audit.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("proof-audit", help="grade, with a model on the Codex CLI, whether "
                                                   "the tests prove each requirement a pull request's Ticket sections "
                                                   "or stored plan define, then run the proof-audit gate on it"))
    _copy_audit.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("copy-audit", help="grade, with a model on the Codex CLI, whether each "
                                                  "sentence and label in a QAE run's copy record is plain English, then "
                                                  "run the copy-audit gate on it"))
    _merge_grant_cli.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("merge-grant", help="whether a merge policy lets an agent merge a "
                                                       "pull request without a human review: prints `eligible: <grant>` "
                                                       "or `not eligible: <reason>` (via gh)"))
    qa_review.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("qa-review", help="post or edit the QAE's one review comment on a pull "
                                                "request (via gh)"))
    qa_limit.add_arguments(commands.add_parser("qa-limit", help="whether every explore job of a run failed on a model "
                                               "usage limit, from their failure annotations (via gh): warns and writes "
                                               "`limited` to $GITHUB_OUTPUT"))
    args = parser.parse_args()
    return COMMANDS[args.command](args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
